result.summary:
The loop-me lesson is accurate, and I found only three low-severity clarity gaps for newcomers.

Facts: every claim about the Skill matches /home/user/skills/snapshot/skills/loop-me/SKILL.md and agents/openai.yaml. The live SKILL.md on main is byte-identical to the snapshot, and loop-me is still in the in-progress bucket. These also match:
- the in-progress README ("Beta", no docs pages, left out of the plugin, `npx skills@latest add mattpocock/skills --skill=<name>`);
- Matt's X post (2026-06-24, quote exact);
- Huntley's Ralph post and Matt's Ralph guide (both quotes exact);
- issue #657 (quotes exact, still closed as not planned when I fetched it today).

The Claude Code claims match the live docs I fetched today (scheduled-tasks, routines, desktop-scheduled-tasks, skills): `/loop 5m check the deploy`, the plain-text block for `disable-model-invocation: true`, "running and idle", the 7-day expiry, the Succeeded/Error/Cancelled meanings, and the Backlog maintenance example. The `--skill` flag and default project scope match the skills CLI reference (v1.7.6). All pinned source URLs return 200 at commit a496f55.

Flagged claim settled: "a scheduled task in Claude's desktop app (which runs on your machine)" is accurate and needs no fix. The docs page is "Schedule recurring tasks in Claude Code Desktop": a local task in the Claude Desktop app's Code tab, under Scheduled, that runs on your machine. The docs offer it as durable scheduling, with no 7-day expiry. The bracket correctly marks it as the local kind, not the cloud kind that the same page also lists.

Questions: each checkpoint, the practice scenario and all 10 final tests have exactly one defensible answer, taken from the lesson text, with correct Why lines and feedback. Leftovers: none (no private projects, old-site features, "this site", numbered references or image pointers). Links: /learn/issues-and-keeping-track, grilling, to-spec, grill-me and skills all exist and cover what they're linked for. The Try it needs only the yoga-studio repo, which "Try it: set up the practice project" in issues-and-keeping-track creates. The validator prints ok.

The three findings: "argument" is never explained; NOTES.md is used before it's introduced, and quick-5 relies on it; GitHub Actions is mentioned once, unexplained and unlinked.
